+# Crack-in-elastic-field fatigue model (single flaw)
+
+This package holds two single-flaw models, scored on the PLA fatigue benchmark (`calibration/pla_fatigue.py`, v1.1.0):
+
+- **ES2020**: a finite-width edge crack, with one constant a0.
+- **YS2025**: a void-seeded crack. Its size grows with the measured void size d_V and saturates at the half-thickness of the infill core.
+
+Every number below is reproduced from a clean clone in under a minute:
+
+```
+pip install numpy scipy                      # plus pytest for the tests
+python -m models.crack_field es              # ES2020, ~1 s
+python -m models.crack_field ys-lin          # YS2025 3D baseline, linear defect, ~6 s
+python -m models.crack_field ys-sat          # YS2025 saturating defect, a_cap = 2.1 mm a priori, ~6 s   <- YS headline
+python -m models.crack_field ys-sat-fit      # YS2025 saturating defect, a_cap fitted, ~40 s
+python -m models.crack_field all --check     # all four: refit + compare with expected.json (exit 1 on mismatch)
+python -m models.crack_field all --stored    # score the stored optima only (no fit), < 2 s
+python -m pytest tests/test_crack_field.py   # 8 tests, ~15 s (+1 slow: the fitted-cap refit)
+```
+
+The inputs are only `calibration/data/pla_fatigue/specimens.csv`, `splits.json`, and two IgBEM field extracts in `fields/extracts/`:
+- `ES_igabem_bisector_profiles.json.gz`
+- `YS_igabem_fields_3d.json.gz`
+
+There are no other files, no network access and no IgBEM solve.
+
+## Equations
+
+**Damage law.** The same law applies to every row of both sources:
+
+    ln c = k [ ln sD − ln(S · F) − γ ln((1 − R)/2) ]
+    ln N_f ~ Normal(ln c, sZ)                     run-outs right-censored (log survival)
+
+- S = `sigma_max_MPa`, the net-section nominal maximum stress. For YS, voids are ignored.
+- `(1 − R)/2` is the Walker term. At R = −1 it equals 1, so γ acts only on R ≥ 0.
+- **Free constants:** sD, k, γ and sZ. Their admissible bounds are 1 < k < 40, 0 ≤ γ ≤ 1 and −4 < ln sZ < 1.5.
+
+**Structural map F.** A crack of depth a sits in the *uncracked* IgBEM field K(x) of the specimen (σ1 per unit net stress, on the notch bisector, at depth x from the root). F is the stress it produces at the process-zone point r = L0/2 ahead of the tip.
+
+**ES2020** (`es`). The plain bar is a uniform field.
+
+    plain:    F = f_sent(a0 / 3.0) / 1.12
+    notched:  F = K̄(a0) · f_sent(a0 / 6.0) / 1.12,        K̄(a) = (2/π) ∫_0^{π/2} K(a sin θ) dθ   (weight-function mean)
+    f_sent(α) = 1.12 − 0.231α + 10.55α² − 21.72α³ + 30.39α⁴    (Tada 2.10, single-edge crack in a strip)
+
+- **Field:** K is the mean of the 5 thickness stations, clipped at the ligament half-width b = 2.997 mm.
+- **Constant:** one, a0, with bounds 1e-3 to 2.9 mm.
+
+**YS2025** (`ys-*`). Here the plain hourglass carries a field too (Kt 1.217).
+
+    F = φ^(−n) [ K(a + r) + (W(a, r) − 1) · K̄(a) ],       W(a, r) = (a + r)/sqrt(r (2a + r))  (Westergaard), r = L0/2
+    F = ∞ if a + r ≥ b = 6.0 mm   (the crack cannot leave the ligament; the wall is tested on the fitted rows only)
+    ys-lin:      a = a0 + κ d_V
+    ys-sat:      a = a0 + a_cap (1 − exp(−κ d_V / a_cap)),   a_cap = 2.1 mm FIXED a priori
+    ys-sat-fit:  same, with a_cap fitted (bounds 0.05 to 5.8 mm)
+
+- **Field:** the mid-plane station z = 2.5 mm, clipped at b. φ is the infill fraction.
+- **Constants:** a0 (0 to 5.5), κ (0 to 30 for linear, 0 to 60 for saturating) and n (0 to 5).
+
+**Fixed constants.**
+- L0 = 0.4 mm.
+- a_cap = 2.1 mm. This is the YS half-thickness 2.5 mm minus the 0.4 mm perimeter shell, i.e. the infill core that a void-seeded crack can occupy. It was chosen before the fit.
+- K̄ is evaluated with 128-point Gauss–Legendre quadrature in θ.
+
+## Protocol
+
+- **Splits:** `paper/ES2020` and `paper/YS2025`. Each fits on plain plus sharp V (every infill level for YS) and holds out U r1 and U r3.
+- **Fit:** maximum likelihood on the FIT ids only. Each start is combined with 2 core starts, and each run is a two-stage Nelder–Mead. `fit()` reports any parameter within 1e-3 of a bound in `on_bound`.
+- **Prediction for each row:** the median strength at that row's life, `S_pred = S · exp((ln c − ln N_f)/k)`.
+- **P10–P90 interval:** `S_pred · exp(±1.2816 sZ / k)`.
+- **Median life:** `exp(ln c)`, passed to the scorer as `pred_life`.
+- **Scoring:** `B.score(pred, "paper", source, interval=..., pred_life=...)`. The run also prints the life-residual statistics: rms, bias, tilt against N_exp and against N_pred, and P10–P90 coverage.
+
+## Expected outputs (`expected.json`)
+
+Stress residuals are log10(S_pred/S_exp), on failures. Life residuals are log10(N_exp/N_pred).
+
+| model | p | AIC | fit rms S | held rms S | net stress, held | held − net (90 % CI, series bootstrap) | fit / held rms log N | held P10–P90 coverage | held tilt vs N_pred (life) |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `es` | 5 | 617.06 | 0.095 | **0.075** | 0.127 | −0.051 [−0.067, −0.033] better | 0.491 / 0.387 | 93 % | −0.05 |
+| `ys-lin` | 7 | 566.18 | 0.072 | 0.088 | 0.197 | −0.109 [−0.139, −0.079] better | 0.355 / 0.434 | 70 % | −0.20 |
+| `ys-sat` | 7 | **543.92** | 0.066 | **0.076** | 0.197 | −0.121 [−0.153, −0.090] better | 0.335 / 0.385 | 74 % | −0.15 |
+| `ys-sat-fit` | 8 | 545.56 | 0.066 | 0.077 | 0.197 | −0.120 [−0.151, −0.089] better | 0.335 / 0.390 | 73 % | −0.16 |
+
+No parameter is on a bound. The fitted constants:
+
+| model | a0 (mm) | κ | n | a_cap (mm) | largest a (mm) | gap to the wall (mm) |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `es` | 0.95 | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a |
+| `ys-lin` | 0.30 | 2.79 | 0.24 | n/a | 4.94 | 0.86 |
+| `ys-sat` | 0.40 | 4.46 | 0.40 | 2.1 fixed | 2.44 | 3.36 |
+| `ys-sat-fit` | 0.39 | 4.38 | 0.38 | 2.41 | 2.69 | 3.11 |
+
+## Departures from the papers (labelled)
+
+- **Not the papers' own methods.** Both papers use a TCD L_M(N). This package replaces that with a crack superposed on the IgBEM field.
+- **YS: Walker exponent γ.** The YS paper uses σ_max only.
+- **YS: a = f(d_V).** The paper uses a homogenised crack a_eq = kt·d_V that shifts plain and notched equally. Here a = f(d_V) is a void-seeded crack with saturation.
+- **YS: plain field.** The plain hourglass field is used, not a uniform bar.
+- **ES: own-lab plain points only** (143 of 195, per `splits.json`), converted with the ASSUMED UTS 42.9 MPa.
+
+## Audits and known limits
+
+**Audit 1** (independent, 2026-11-16). The shared field code mirrored the profile with no cap on a, which gave a spurious likelihood mode at a ≈ 23 mm. The old 24-point quadrature was also 2–3 % low for V. Both are fixed here: the field is clipped at b, the wall is a + L0/2 < b, and the quadrature is 128-point Gauss.
+
+**Audit 2.** It confirmed the fix and found a sign error in the old stress-bias column; this has been corrected. The finite-width Irwin factor on the YS crack costs +34 AIC, so it is off.
+
+**Audit 3** (saturating defect, 80 random starts, own loader and scorer). It reproduced the AIC to 1e-3 and every score to 1e-4. It found three things:
+- **Held-out leak.** The wall check covered the held-out rows. It is now limited to fitted rows; the optima are unchanged.
+- **a0 kinks.** a0 sits on a K-profile node because the field is linearly interpolated, so its curvature standard errors are meaningless. A smooth interpolant changes AIC by at most 0.01.
+- **a_cap profile.** The profile minimum is at 2.4 mm, with an approximate 95 % interval of 1.7–4.0. Fixing a_cap = 2.1 costs 0.18 NLL.
+
+**Caveats a validator should test:**
+- **The saturation evidence depends on the 20 % infill level.** Leaving out 20 % gives ΔAIC(sat − lin) = +1.6. Leaving out any other level gives −10 to −24. The held-out gain shows at every level but is about 0 at 60 % and small at 80 %. The held-out set prefers a smaller a_cap than the fitted set.
+- **YS U r1 − U r3 gap.** The mean life residual differs by −0.27 decades and grows with d_V (−0.20 at 100 %, −0.47 at 20 %). Per-level biases reach +0.40 decades at 100 % and −0.31 at 40 %. This is the main open deficiency.
+- **Coverage below target.** Held-out P10–P90 coverage is 70–74 % on YS (target 80 %). The benchmark's stress-tilt against N_pred flags YS held-out as TILTED (+0.03 per decade, t 3.3).
+- **Identifiability.** With only plain + V fitted, γ and any R or gradient driver only change how U is interpolated.
+- **ES plain geometry** is assumed (33 mm gauge, 3 mm thick). The plain strip width used by f_sent (3 mm per side) is part of that assumption.
+
+## Provenance
+
+The model was developed in IgBEM (`Fakasht10/Igabem-elasticity-`, branch `claude/project-thread-ramlnd`, `python/fatigue/ys2025_audit/`). That branch holds:
+- the original scripts;
+- audits 1–3 (`audit3/`: refit, profile, robustness, smooth);
+- `bench/bench_rescore.json`, the first rescore on benchmark 3f2a5d5.
+
+This package is a clean re-implementation on the benchmark loader. It reproduces those numbers exactly.
